\]<br>Here \( V=100 \) Volt<br>Therefore, \( \lambda=\frac{12.27}{\sqrt{100}}=\frac{12.27}{10}=1.227 \mathrm{AA} \)

Given \( \vec{F}=5 \hat{i}+2 \hat{j}-5 \hat{k} ; \vec{r}=\hat{i}-2 \hat{j}+\hat{k} \)<br>We know torque<br>\[
\tau=\vec{r} \times \vec{F}=\left|\begin{array}{ccc}
\hat{i} & \hat{j} & \hat{k} \\
1 & -2 & 1 \\
5 & 2 & -5
\end{array}\right|
\]<br>Therefore,<br>\[
\begin{array}{l}
\tau=\hat{i}(10-2)-\hat{j}(-5-5)+\hat{k}(2+10) \\
\Rightarrow \tau=8 \hat{i}+10 \hat{j}+12 \hat{k}
\end{array}
\]<br>Thus, torque about origin is \( 8 \hat{i}+10 \hat{j}+12 \hat{k} \)

When light of suitable frequency falls on metal plate electrons are emitted. This effect is called photoelectric effect.<br>The maximum kinetic energy of the photo electrons depends only on frequency of the incident light. The relation is given<br>as<br>\[
K_{\max }=h f=h f_{0}
\]<br>where \( f_{0} \) is threshold frequency; \( \mathrm{f} \) is frequency of incident light.

Given, speed \( =30 \mathrm{~ms}^{-1} \), angle of projection \( =45^{\circ} ; g=10 \mathrm{~ms}^{-2} \)<br>Maximum height reached by the stone is<br>\[
\begin{array}{l}
H=\frac{v^{2} \sin ^{2} \theta}{2 g}=\frac{(30)^{2}(\sin 45)^{2}}{2 \times 10}=\frac{900 \times \frac{1}{2}}{2 \times 10}=\frac{900 \times 1}{2 \times 2 \times 10} \\
\Rightarrow H=\frac{90}{4}=22.5
\end{array}
\]<br>Therefore, maximum height reached by stone is \( 22.5 \mathrm{~m} \).

Given, length of wire, \( I=1 \mathrm{~m} ; \) mass of wire, \( m=10 \times 10^{-3} \mathrm{~kg} \) tension, \( T=100 \mathrm{~N} \)<br>Speed of transverse wave, \( v=\sqrt{\frac{\text { Tension }}{\text { linear density }}} \)<br>Linear density<br>\[
\begin{array}{l}
=\frac{\text { mass }}{\text { length }}=\frac{10 \times 10^{-3}}{1}=10 \times 10^{-3} \\
\Rightarrow v=\sqrt{\frac{100}{10 \times 10^{-3}}}=\sqrt{10^{4}}=100 \mathrm{~ms}^{-1}
\end{array}
\]<br>Therefore, speed of transverse wave \( =100 \mathrm{~ms}^{-1} \)
